-- title:
--   Integrality over K[j] passes to L[j] in a tower
-- statement:
--   Let $K$, $L$ and $F$ be commutative rings equipped with algebra structures making $F$ an algebra over $L$, $L$ an algebra over $K$ and $F$ an algebra over $K$, with the three structure maps compatible (an `IsScalarTower K L F` hypothesis: scalar multiplication by $K$ on $F$ factors through $L$). Let $j$ and $x$ be elements of $F$. The hypothesis is that $x$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ K\ \{j\}$ of $F$ generated by $j$, that is, $x$ is a root of a monic polynomial with coefficients in that subalgebra. The conclusion is that $x$ is likewise integral over the $L$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ L\ \{j\}$ of $F$ generated by $j$. No finiteness, reducedness or domain assumptions are imposed on any of the three rings.
--
--   This is the elementary transitivity observation that enlarging the ring of scalars from $K[j]$ to $L[j]$ inside $F$ preserves integrality; in the modular-curve part of the development it is used to pass from integrality over $\mathbb{Q}[j]$ to integrality over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}[j]$, and it is invoked in the study of $q$-expansions, of finite-dimensionality of algebras generated by $j(q)$, and of cuspidal classes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_isIntegral_adjoin_of_isScalarTower.lean

import Mathlib.RingTheory.IntegralClosure.IsIntegral.Basic

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
